nodecode-belive-aihub is a rewrite of belivetech's belivetech/hermes-belive-aihub:

  https://github.com/belivetech/hermes-belive-aihub
  commit 5d022b5a7190c673162965f4593b5a86f14e2c84
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 BeLive Technology (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the provider profile — the `belive-aihub` id, its OpenAI-compatible
base and its staging base, the `BELIVE_TOKENHUB_API_KEY` workspace key, and the
`chat_completions` wire — and the part that gives the plugin its point: the
picker is filled from the hub's own model list, keeping only the rows whose
catalog says text output, tool support, and a ready Chat Completions endpoint
that takes tools, with the plugin's `fallback_models` answering when that list
is unreachable and a successful empty answer kept empty. The plugin's 4 MiB
catalog ceiling and 8-second deadline ride along. In Nodecode the profile is a
row in the models.dev catalog table, two hooks and a worker that fetches the
catalog once at start.

Left out: the Hermes-side plumbing, which has no Nodecode counterpart and no
bearing on what a user gets — `register_provider`, the `ProviderProfile`
dataclass and its fields, the `fetch_models` and `live_context_length` hooks
(Nodecode resolves a model's context window from the same catalog row it lists
the model from, so no second hook restates it), and the plugin's
`default_aux_model` and `default_vision_model` (Nodecode picks its own
auxiliary model, and offers an image to the models whose catalog says they read
one). The plugin's custom no-redirect opener is not restated either: Nodecode's
one HTTP verb takes no redirect policy, and discovery is sent only to the hub's
two official HTTPS bases. The repository's `hermes plugins install` advice,
`VALIDATION.md`, the qualification record and the Python test suite are the
plugin's own, and are not carried.
